---
import Flower from "../../components/Flower.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const lang = "en";

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/",
        name: "Lilo Browser",
        position: 1,
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/",
        name: "Arc alternative",
        position: 2,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/arc-alternative/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo Browser: a free Arc alternative for Mac, without Chromium"
  description="Looking for an Arc alternative? Lilo Browser keeps Spaces, the sidebar, split view and Peek, imports your Arc spaces, and runs on WebKit instead of Chromium. Free and open source."
  schema={schema}
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <div class="top">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <Flower id="hero-flower" class="mark bloom" />
          <h1>
            An Arc alternative
            <br />
            <span>that keeps going.</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="sub">
            The Browser Company shuts Arc down on December 22, 2026, to focus on
            Dia. Lilo keeps what you liked about Arc, Spaces in a sidebar, split
            view, Peek and a little window, and brings your Arc spaces with you.
            It is free, open source, and built on WebKit instead of Chromium.
          </p>
          <div class="cta">
            <a
              class="pill dark"
              href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/releases/latest/download/Lilo.dmg"
            >
              Download for Mac
            </a>
            <a class="pill soft" href="#compare">
              Compare with Arc
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="band" id="familiar">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <span class="tag">What feels familiar</span>
          <h2>The Arc way of browsing, still here.</h2>
          <p>
            The ideas that made Arc different are the ones Lilo is built around.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-list two">
          <div>
            <b>Spaces.</b> Keep work, personal and private browsing apart, each
            with its name, color, emoji and, if you like, its own profile with
            its own sign-ins and extensions.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>A sidebar.</b> Tabs on the left like Arc, or on the right, top or
            bottom. Pinned tabs, favorites and folders you can nest and lock.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Split view.</b> Up to four pages side by side, or a terminal
            beside your work.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Peek and the Little window.</b> Links from pinned tabs open over
            the page, and a quick window from any app takes one link or one
            search, then goes to a Space if it turns into something.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Command bar.</b> Tabs, bookmarks, history and every action from
            one field, with <kbd>⌘T</kbd>.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Tab archive.</b> Unpinned tabs clear themselves into an archive,
            with a delay you set per Space, and come back when you need them.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Your shortcuts and link rules.</b> Start from the Arc preset in
            Settings, then remap anything. Rules send links from other apps to
            the Space you pick, like Air Traffic Control.
          </div>
          <div>
            <b>Your extensions.</b> Manifest V3 extensions from the Chrome Web
            Store install with one click and keep updating.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="compare">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <span class="tag">Side by side</span>
          <h2 id="arc-comparison">Arc and Lilo, compared.</h2>
          <p>Two sidebar browsers for the Mac, built in very different ways.</p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="matrix"
          role="region"
          aria-labelledby="arc-comparison"
          tabindex="0"
        >
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <td></td>
                <th scope="col">Arc</th>
                <th scope="col">Lilo</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Engine</th>
                <td>Chromium</td>
                <td>WebKit, the engine Safari uses</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">New features</th>
                <td class="n">Shuts down December 22, 2026</td>
                <td class="y">Public beta, regular releases</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Price</th>
                <td>Free</td>
                <td>Free</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Source code</th>
                <td class="n">Closed</td>
                <td class="y">Open source, MIT</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Account</th>
                <td>Required</td>
                <td>None</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Spaces and profiles</th>
                <td class="y">Yes</td>
                <td class="y">Yes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Chrome Web Store extensions</th>
                <td class="y">Yes</td>
                <td class="y">Manifest V3</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Terminal tabs</th>
                <td class="n">No</td>
                <td class="y">Yes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">AI agents</th>
                <td class="n">No MCP server</td>
                <td class="y">Local MCP server, opt-in</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Sync</th>
                <td class="y">Yes</td>
                <td class="n">No</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Platforms</th>
                <td>Mac, Windows</td>
                <td>Mac only, macOS 26 or later</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="move">
      <div class="wrap steps">
        <div class="head" style="margin:0">
          <span class="tag">Moving from Arc</span>
          <h2>Your Arc spaces come with you.</h2>
          <p>
            Lilo reads Arc on your Mac and changes nothing in it.{" "}
            <a href="/moving/">Everything about moving to Lilo</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="claims">
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#b39df4">
              1
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Spaces, tabs and splits</h3>
              <p>
                Each Arc space becomes a Lilo Space with its icon and color, its
                pinned and open tabs, folders and splits of up to four tabs. Arc
                favorites become bookmarked tabs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#9ac7eb">
              2
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>History and sign-ins</h3>
              <p>
                Your most recent pages fill the command bar, and your cookies
                are copied so you stay signed in. macOS asks once to let Lilo
                use Arc's "Safe Storage" key.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="ic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
              3
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3>Extensions</h3>
              <p>
                The ones from the Chrome Web Store are installed again from the
                store, up to date. You pick which.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="faq">
      <div class="wrap faq">
        <div class="head" style="margin:0">
          <span class="tag">Questions</span>
          <h2>Leaving Arc.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>Is Arc being discontinued?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. The Browser Company stopped building new features for Arc in
              2025 to focus on Dia, and
              <a href="https://x.com/arcinternet/status/2113301454597640570">
                announced
              </a>
              that it shuts Arc down on December 22, 2026. Lilo reads your Arc
              spaces straight from your Mac, so import them while Arc is still
              installed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is Lilo based on Chromium, like Arc?</summary>
            <p>
              No. Lilo is built on WebKit, the engine that ships with macOS, in
              an app written in Swift. No Chromium is bundled, which keeps it
              light on memory and battery, and it still runs Chrome extensions.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I import my Arc spaces?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. Each space comes in as a Lilo Space with its icon, color,
              pinned and open tabs, folders and splits. Anything in Arc's
              sidebar that is not a tab, a folder or a split is skipped.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Will my Arc keyboard shortcuts work?</summary>
            <p>
              Pick the Arc preset in Settings, General, Shortcuts, then remap
              anything you like. Conflicts are flagged as you type.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What does Lilo not have that Arc has?</summary>
            <p>
              Sync, a Windows version and a mobile app. Lilo has no account and
              no server, so there is nothing to sync through. Your password
              manager keeps syncing your passwords everywhere.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Could Lilo shut down like Arc?</summary>
            <p>
              Not the way Arc does. Lilo is MIT licensed, so the app and its
              code stay yours to run and fork, and there is no server or account
              that could be switched off. The engine is the WebKit that ships
              with macOS, so Apple's security fixes keep arriving with macOS
              updates. Lilo is made by Alexis Rouillard, who has also maintained
              the open-source Waybar since 2018.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is Lilo free?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. Lilo is free and open source under the{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/LICENSE">
                MIT license
              </a>
              . There is no paid tier, no account and no telemetry.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap end">
        <span class="tag">Free and open source</span>
        <Flower id="end-flower" class="mark small-mark" />
        <h2>Try it beside Arc.</h2>
        <p>
          Import your spaces, keep Arc around as long as you like, and see if
          Lilo feels like home.
        </p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center">
          <a
            class="pill dark"
            href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/releases/latest/download/Lilo.dmg"
            style="padding:14px 22px"
          >
            Download for Mac
          </a>
          <a
            class="pill soft"
            href="/"
            style="padding:14px 22px;background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 30%, var(--surface))"
          >
            Back to Lilo
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
